# LitClock Control PWA — scoped sudo for system actions (issue litclock-dev#245 M4 + M5).
#
# This file documents the narrow set of privileged commands the Control PWA
# needs. /etc/sudoers.d/010_pi-nopasswd grants `pi` NOPASSWD: ALL, and
# pi-gen/stage3/02-configure-system/00-run.sh still writes it on every image.
#
# 010 IS KEPT, DELIBERATELY — the drop is not pending work. It was planned
# under litclock-dev#387 (and bundled into litclock-dev#82) and then formally REVERSED by the owner on
# 2026-07-12: with a known default password a scoped sudo blocks only
# accidental escalation, not RCE (`sudo -S`), while the drop is a brick-risky
# refactor of first-boot/update/LKG. litclock-dev#387 closed 2026-07-12 on that
# ratification and litclock-dev#82 closed 2026-07-14 with the image-hardening bundle
# explicitly removed from scope. The question reopens only if a non-default
# per-device password ever lands (or `pi` leaves the `sudo` group) — at that
# point the password becomes a real boundary. Nothing in this file is waiting
# on that.
#
# SCOPE — this allowlist covers ONLY control_server's own sudo call sites
# (litclock-dev#387 closed those escalation paths). It does NOT cover the rest of the
# `User=pi` runtime: update.sh (~60 sudo calls), first-boot.sh (~26),
# wifi_provision.py, litclock-bootcheck.sh, and the splash/LKG scripts invoke
# many commands that are NOT granted here (apt-get, cp, install, mkdir, rm, mv,
# chown, tee, nft, nmcli, rfkill, sysctl, usermod, visudo, systemctl
# daemon-reload/enable/disable, systemd-tmpfiles, …).
#
# That gap is why the reversal matters operationally, and it is also the
# standing reason to keep adding scoped entries here rather than leaning on
# 010: those scripts run non-interactively (timer- and boot-driven), so a
# password-requiring sudo cannot prompt — it fails at each call site. update.sh
# deliberately omits `set -e` and several calls end in `2>/dev/null || true`, so
# a pi whose blanket grant went away would no-op every privileged step of an OTA
# update and still report SUCCESS, fleet-wide, while litclock-bootcheck.sh (the
# brick-recovery auto-revert) degrades the same way. A scoped 020 grant is the
# right default for anything that does not genuinely need the blanket one; the
# binary-level coverage gate sketched on litclock-dev#548 would be the
# prerequisite if the posture were ever revisited.
#
# Commands MUST exactly match what the caller runs (sudoers matches the
# binary path + arguments verbatim). On Bookworm `which systemctl` is
# /usr/bin/systemctl. Both bare and `--no-block` forms are listed because
# the control_server uses --no-block (so the HTTP response flushes before
# the box reboots) and other call sites may not.
#
# M5 entries:
#   * `systemctl start --no-block litclock-update.service` — /api/update/apply
#     (D1: systemd's job queue serializes against the Sunday timer fire).
#   * `systemctl start --no-block litclock-wifi-reset.service` — /api/wifi/reset
#     (D11: replaces the daemon-thread approach; Conflicts= directive on the
#     wifi-reset unit gives systemd-native job-level interlock against
#     reset-during-update).
#
# Issue litclock-dev#280 entry:
#   * `systemctl start --no-block litclock-prepare-for-gift.service` —
#     /api/system/prepare-for-gift. The unit's ExecStart is reset-setup.sh
#     --gift-mode --message-file /run/litclock/gift-message; the message
#     itself is written to /run/litclock/gift-message by the route handler
#     before invoking systemctl, so the sudoers entry doesn't need to
#     accept arbitrary message text on the command line.
#
# Issue litclock-dev#510 entry:
#   * `systemctl start --no-block litclock-reset.service` — /api/system/reset
#     (Factory reset). The unit's ExecStart is reset-setup.sh --wipe-wifi
#     --strict-env-wipe --poweroff --yes (full wipe + power off for pack-up,
#     litclock-dev#627). Fixed argv, no user input crosses the sudo boundary. Sibling of
#     the wifi-reset/gift grants.
#
# EPIC litclock-dev#383 PR2 (litclock-dev#388) entry:
#   * `touch /etc/litclock/.handoff-complete` — control_server's handoff
#     completion (POST /api/handoff/done, /set-timezone, the 120s timer, and
#     an implicit Settings-save complete). /etc/litclock is root-owned, so the
#     pi-user control_server writes this marker via sudo. Fixed path + binary,
#     no arguments, so there's nothing user-controllable on the command line.
#     See src/control_server/handoff.py:mark_handoff_complete.
#
# litclock-dev#396 entry:
#   * `timedatectl set-timezone UTC` — prepare_for_gift resets the system tz to
#     UTC synchronously (so the gifter's tz doesn't linger in /etc/localtime)
#     before dispatching the teardown unit. Fixed argv (UTC is the only target),
#     so nothing user-controllable crosses the sudo boundary. Best-effort: the
#     route swallows failure (see system.py:_gift_reset_timezone_to_utc), so a
#     missing entry degrades to the script's Step 3.5 reset, never a hard error.
#
# litclock-dev#387 entries (020-completion). These shipped as defense-in-depth and stay
# shipped after the 2026-07-12 reversal — they closed real pi->root escalation
# paths regardless of 010, and they are the scoped default for call sites that
# do not need the blanket grant:
#   * `/usr/bin/timedatectl set-ntp true` — first-boot.sh (User=pi in
#     litclock-firstboot.service) enables NTP during provisioning.
#     Fixed argv (`set-ntp true` is the only form invoked). Before litclock-dev#387 this
#     worked ONLY via 010's blanket grant, so a fresh clock's time sync hung
#     off the broad file for no reason. Surfaced by the litclock-dev#387 security /review.
#   * `/usr/local/lib/litclock/litclock-set-timezone` — the ARBITRARY-tz path
#     (geocoding.set_system_timezone: IP-geo + the A18 browser-tz fallback).
#     We authorize the root-owned wrapper's FIXED PATH, never `timedatectl
#     set-timezone *` (a glob would let pi set the clock to any string reaching
#     the resolver). The wrapper re-validates the tz against the kernel zoneinfo
#     list in root-owned code, so any argument pi passes is safe. Installed
#     root:root 0755 so pi cannot rewrite it. Before litclock-dev#387 this path worked ONLY
#     via 010_pi-nopasswd's blanket grant; the scoped entry is what makes it
#     auditable, which is worth having whether or not 010 is present.
#
# litclock-dev#847 item 5 entries — the ONE deliberate exception to the scope
# warning above:
#   * `/usr/bin/systemctl start litclock.timer` — update.sh Phase 7 and both
#     revert arms re-arm the clock after Phase 1 stopped it.
#   * `/usr/bin/systemctl start --no-block litclock.timer` — update.sh's
#     EXIT/signal trap (`timeout -k 5 10 sudo -n systemctl start --no-block
#     litclock.timer`, litclock-dev#835), the re-arm that runs when the update
#     is killed mid-way. Every other update.sh sudo call degrades to a
#     no-op'd step and a loud status; THIS one degrading is a dark panel until
#     a power-cycle, so it gets its own scoped grant rather than riding
#     010_pi-nopasswd's blanket one. The bare and
#     --no-block forms are both listed because sudoers matches argv verbatim.
#     tests/test_sudoers_m5.py derives both from update.sh's executed lines.
#
# Validate any change with:  visudo -c -f sudoers/020_litclock-control
# Install via: scripts/update.sh or the pi-gen image build
# (pi-gen/stage3/02-configure-system/00-run.sh).

pi ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl reboot, /usr/bin/systemctl reboot --no-block, /usr/bin/systemctl poweroff, /usr/bin/systemctl poweroff --no-block, /usr/bin/systemctl restart litclock.service, /usr/bin/systemctl restart litclock.timer, /usr/bin/systemctl start litclock.service, /usr/bin/systemctl start --no-block litclock.service, /usr/bin/systemctl start litclock.timer, /usr/bin/systemctl start --no-block litclock.timer, /usr/bin/systemctl start --no-block litclock-update.service, /usr/bin/systemctl start --no-block litclock-wifi-reset.service, /usr/bin/systemctl start --no-block litclock-prepare-for-gift.service, /usr/bin/systemctl start --no-block litclock-reset.service, /usr/bin/systemctl stop litclock.timer litclock.service, /usr/bin/touch /etc/litclock/.handoff-complete, /usr/bin/timedatectl set-timezone UTC, /usr/bin/timedatectl set-ntp true, /usr/local/lib/litclock/litclock-set-timezone
